 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:17:11.373941Z",
 "text": "{\n  \"verdict\": \"void\",\n  \"section\": \"119\",\n  \"quote\": \"A landlord who is lawfully entitled to increase the rent charged to a tenant for a rental unit may do so only if at least 12 months have elapsed,\",\n  \"reason\": \"Under section 119, a landlord may only increase rent if at least 12 months have elapsed since the previous increase or since the unit was first rented. Because this statutory requirement cannot be contracted out of, a clause permitting rent increases every six months is inconsistent with the Act and void.\"\n}",
